course: dictionary
lesson: 7588
title: Price
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A price is how much money you must pay to get something. «The bread costs 80p» tells you the price. Prices are written in pounds (£) and pence (p), and «£1 is 100p» helps you compare them.
explain: Price can also be a verb. To price something is to decide or mark how much it costs, which is why shops put price tags on things. «Half price» means you pay half of the normal price.
explain: Price can mean something bad you must accept to get something else. In «the price of fame», there is no money at all: it means the hard things you give up to become famous. Check by swapping in the word money. If it does not fit, the meaning is something else.
fact: A price is the money you must pay to buy something.
fact: «£1 is 100p, which is 10 times 10p.»
fact: Inflation is when the prices of most things rise over time.
fact: The cost of living is how much money you need for food, heating, rent and other everyday things.
fact: Some taxes hide inside prices: VAT is «£2 of a £12 toy» in the UK.
exercise: «Half price» on a toy means what? | The toy is free for ever | *You pay half the normal price | The toy is broken | You pay twice the normal price
exercise: In «the price of fame», what does price mean? | A kind of coin | A sign on a shop window | *Something hard you must give up to get it | An amount of money you pay in a shop
exercise: «A price far below normal for a popular new item» can be a warning sign of what? | Free delivery every time | A bigger, busier shop | *Fake goods or goods that never arrive | A bargain that is always safe
test: To price something means what? | To pay for it with a card | *To decide or mark how much it costs | To paint it a bright colour | To take it back to the shop
test: Inflation is when what happens? | *The prices of most things rise over time | Shops close early on Sundays | Money stops working for ever | Every price falls to zero
exam: Which phrase means the money you need for food, heating, rent and other everyday things? | *Cost of living | Income tax | Price tag | Inflation
